;;; A real `sigilcoin` process, for the listener tests.
;;;
;;; `test-cli.sgl` cannot both run `sigilcoin listen` and attack it: the
;;; accept loop owns the process it runs in, and the hostile connections
;;; have to arrive while it is accepting. So the listener goes in its own
;;; operating-system process and this file is what that process runs.
;;;
;;; It is the `sigilcoin` binary's own entry point and nothing more, so the
;;; listener under attack is the one an operator deploys:
;;;
;;; ```sh
;;; sigil -L <paths...> listen-peer.sgl -- listen --regtest --data-dir DIR
;;; ```
;;;
;;; `main` takes the words after `--`, which is how the toolchain separates
;;; its own arguments from an application's, so the `-L` module paths the
;;; test passes through never reach the CLI.
;;;
;;; This is not a test file: the runner collects `test-*.sgl` and
;;; `*-test.sgl`, so it is never executed as one.

(import (sigil coin cli))

(main)
